::CW_U05_PRE_Q01::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Draft problem: A peer says the ending is unclear because the reader cannot tell why the character leaves. What is the best next step?{
~A. Change every character name.#Incorrect. Names do not address motivation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Add or revise a moment that shows the character's reason for leaving.#Correct. This responds directly to the feedback. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Ignore the feedback because all endings are confusing.#Incorrect. Feedback should be considered. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Add random punctuation.#Incorrect. Punctuation will not clarify motivation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q02::[MLA.CW.REV.02] Workshop context: Which feedback is most useful?{
~A. I do not like stories like this.#Incorrect. This is personal preference. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. It is good.#Incorrect. This is too vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Change everything.#Incorrect. This is too broad. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. The repeated hallway image builds tension; consider clarifying what the character fears at the end.#Correct. This names a strength and a focused suggestion.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q03::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Format context: A script has dialogue but no character labels. What should be revised?{
=A. Add clear speaker labels and useful stage directions.#Correct. This supports script readability. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Remove all dialogue.#Incorrect. Dialogue is central to script form. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Add random stanza breaks.#Incorrect. Stanza breaks do not solve script speaker clarity.
~D. Ignore formatting.#Incorrect. Format matters. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q04::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which answer best explains author craft reasoning?{
~A. I like my poem.#Incorrect. This is opinion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The poem has words.#Incorrect. This is too general. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. I placed the final image on its own line so the reader pauses before the speaker's decision.#Correct. This names a craft choice and reader effect.
~D. I wrote it quickly.#Incorrect. Speed is not craft reasoning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q05::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which piece is most publication-ready?{
~A. A rough draft with no title.#Incorrect. It lacks readiness checks. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A script with no speaker labels.#Incorrect. This has a format issue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A story with random spacing and no review.#Incorrect. Random spacing and no review weaken readiness.
=D. A revised poem with purposeful line breaks, checked conventions, clear title, and appropriate content.#Correct. This includes revision, format, conventions, and audience readiness.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q06::[MLA.CW.COM.02] Feedback says a paragraph is confusing because two speakers are in one paragraph. Which revision best fits?{
~A. Add more unrelated setting.#Incorrect. The issue is speaker clarity. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Start a new paragraph when the speaker changes and check dialogue tags.#Correct. This improves clarity and format. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Change the title only.#Incorrect. The title does not solve dialogue confusion.
~D. Delete all dialogue.#Incorrect. A targeted revision is better. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q07::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which answer should be eliminated first?{
~A. Consider feedback and protect the writer's purpose.#Incorrect. This is strong. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Explain reader effect.#Incorrect. Reader effect is important. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Accept every suggestion automatically.#Correct. Automatic acceptance can damage purpose and voice.
~D. Check format before publication.#Incorrect. Format checks are important. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q08::[MLA.CW.REV.02] A portfolio note should include what?{
=A. Selection reason, revision evidence, and final readiness explanation.#Correct. This supports portfolio readiness. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Only "I like it."#Incorrect. This is too vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Private personal information.#Incorrect. That is inappropriate. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A list of random colors.#Incorrect. That does not explain writing growth. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q09::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which is the best author presentation opening?{
~A. My story has stuff in it.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. "Open Door" is a short story meant to show a character deciding whether to trust someone again.#Correct. This names title, form, and purpose. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. I will tell the whole plot first.#Incorrect. This over-summarizes. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I do not know why I wrote it.#Incorrect. This lacks purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q10::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which revision most directly improves reader effect?{
~A. Changing the font randomly.#Incorrect. This does not improve the writing itself.
~B. Deleting the title for no reason.#Incorrect. That may weaken reader preparation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Ignoring feedback about clarity.#Incorrect. That does not improve reader effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Replacing "I was sad" with an image of the last bus passing without stopping.#Correct. This shows emotion through image. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}
